  "ai-unit-planning": {
    title: "AI Unit Planning",
    summary: "Toddle case study on unit template customization, unified planner fields, dataset mapping, and early AI-assisted unit creation workflows for curriculum planning.",
    body: "> Building a more flexible unit planning system for curriculum templates, custom datasets, and AI-assisted planner creation.\n\n---\n\n## Introduction\n\nAt Toddle, unit planning sits at the center of how schools plan curriculum across programs like PYP, MYP, DP, and UbD. Each curriculum has its own unit templates. Each template is made of fields, and those fields can either be dataset-driven or custom-created by the school.\n\nThe dataset-driven fields had three important dataset types: card datasets, checklist datasets, and nested checklist datasets. Whenever an admin created a card, checklist, or nested checklist field, it needed to be tagged to a dataset of the same type. That dataset then powered the options teachers used inside the unit planner.\n\nMy work on this project was split into three phases: unifying the existing planner fields, redesigning the unit template customization experience, and exploring how AI could help teachers generate meaningful customized unit planners.\n\n## How I Framed The Work\n\nI started the project by writing a working cover note in Coda so everyone could understand why this work mattered before getting into the tables. The core problem was simple: teachers spend a lot of time planning units that need to align with different curriculum structures. When the planning flow is inconsistent or hard to configure, it creates extra work for teachers and makes it harder for schools to maintain curriculum quality.\n\nThe first users I considered were teachers, students, and families. Teachers need to create and deliver strong unit plans. Students need a clear learning path. Families need enough visibility to understand what their child is learning and how they can support them.\n\nFrom there, I translated the work into four use cases:\n\n| User | Need | Design question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Teacher creating a unit | Generate unit plans quickly so they can spend more time teaching. | How might we help teachers generate curriculum-aligned unit plans using AI? |\n| Teacher collaborating with colleagues | Co-create and refine unit plans with shared best practices. | How might AI support collaboration without removing teacher ownership? |\n| Student using a unit plan | Understand the learning path and stay engaged. | How might unit plans become easier for students to access and follow? |\n| Family viewing curriculum | See enough curriculum context to support learning at home. | How might families get useful visibility without adding admin work for teachers? |\n\nThis framing helped me keep the project sequential: first understand the system, then simplify the components, then redesign customization, and only then decide where AI should enter the workflow.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe unit planning system had grown across multiple curricula, template types, and component patterns. Similar fields were implemented in different ways, which made maintenance difficult and created inconsistencies between templates.\n\nAt the same time, Toddle was moving toward a more flexible single-page unit planner. That meant the admin-side customization experience also needed to evolve. Schools needed a clearer way to configure fields, connect them to datasets, understand dependencies, and decide which parts of a unit planner should be fixed, editable, duplicated, or AI-assisted.\n\nThe AI workflow added another layer of complexity: before generating a useful unit plan, we first had to understand which fields were required, which were optional, and what minimum context an AI system needed to produce a meaningful output.\n\n## Coda References I Used\n\nI used Coda as the working space for breaking down the product problem. The most useful references were not just screenshots; they were tables that helped me compare templates, fields, datasets, and AI feasibility.\n\n| Coda reference | What I used it for | How it shaped the product direction |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Problem statement and use cases | Defined the teacher, student, and family needs before designing AI. | Kept the project focused on planning quality, not just AI generation. |\n| Project Pipeline | Tracked AI unit creation tasks by status, month, week, and planning step. | Helped split the AI work into unit outline generation, editing actions, entry points, template coverage, and impacted areas. |\n| Template-wise fields | Compared PYP, MYP, DP, and UbD planner fields. | Helped identify repeated field patterns that could be unified. |\n| UI Analysis | Compared fields by importance, AI input requirement, AI feasibility, required parameters, and output format. | Helped decide which fields were required for AI and which could be optional. |\n| UI Explanation | Mapped current field components to the new input UI. | Became the base for the unified field component model. |\n| Admin dataset changes | Mapped curriculum-specific items into card, checklist, nested checklist, and dependent dataset types. | Helped define how admin datasets should power template fields. |\n| Discussion points | Captured product decisions around editable templates, official datasets, dependencies, bulk upload, and migration risk. | Turned open questions into scope boundaries for the first version. |\n| AI agents in LMS | Explored where AI agents could support content, students, assessment, admin work, and personalization. | Kept the unit-planning AI workflow connected to the broader LMS direction. |\n\n## Phase 1: Unifying Unit Planner Fields\n\nI started by auditing the fields used across curriculum planners and mapping them into a smaller unified component system. The goal was to reduce multiple one-off implementations into a consistent field model that could work across unit templates.\n\nThe unified field model included common inputs like rich text, text area, single-line text, list, reflection box, card, checklist, and nested checklist fields. Dataset-backed fields were treated separately from free custom fields because they required stronger rules around tagging, data structure, dependencies, and reuse.\n\nThis phase also helped clarify which fields could support AI generation. For example, unit title, theme, and central idea could become required or high-signal inputs, while fields like cover images could be generated later as supporting content. The field audit became the foundation for deciding what AI could generate, what teachers should control manually, and what information was required before the system could create a useful planner.\n\n### Field audit references\n\nIn the template-wise field tables, I compared planner fields across curricula. For example, in the MYP subject-specific planner, fields like approaches to learning, summative assessment, content, subject standards, learning experiences, formative assessment, differentiation, service learning, and description appeared with input types such as rich text editor, checkbox in modal, selection card, and checkbox on page.",
  },
